$DIR/main.p:10:10: cannot pass HF((func() int literal)) to call, which keeps it after returning
    HF((func() int literal)) is a function literal that uses x. A function
    literal that uses variables of the function around it refers to those
    variables where they live, in f's frame (x is declared at main.p:9). call
    passes s to s.String (which may call main.(HF).String) (main.p:7), which
    stores h in package variable keepF (main.p:6), so HF((func() int literal))
    would outlive x.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change call to take a value or an owner, which it can keep: an owned[T] from
    new, a shared[T], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
